1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This tutorial explains the extra feature in Nesta, our online job fair registration project.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A signed-in student can download their own interview schedule as a CSV file. We

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
will build the protected route, query only that student's bookings, format the file, and

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
verify the result. The examples use our actual source code and recorded local API

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
responses. This walkthrough uses synthetic narration.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
First install the locked dependencies, then start the demo server. This command starts a

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
temporary local MongoDB and creates fictional companies and demo accounts. Log in as Alice

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
and retain the returned token. These published credentials are for disposable rehearsal data only.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
For the persistent project environment, configure your authorized MongoDB Atlas connection and use your

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
own credentials. Never place a real secret in the repository.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
In routes slash bookings, the router first applies protect. The export handler is registered

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
before the colon id route. This order matters: otherwise Express could interpret the word

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
export as a booking identifier. We keep the same route, middleware, controller structure taught

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
in the course. No separate frontend or new authentication system is necessary. An unauthenticated

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
export request must be rejected before querying any schedule data.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Next, query bookings using request dot user dot id. Protect has already verified the

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
token and loaded this user. Do not accept an owner identifier from the query

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
string or request body, because a client could replace it with somebody else's identifier.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Populate retrieves the company contact fields from the reference. Sort by interview date and

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
identifier so the exported schedule has a stable order.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Start with a header row containing the interview date and the four company contact

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
columns. For every booking, convert the stored date to the first ten characters of

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
its ISO representation. Our API stores these date-only values at UTC midnight, so this

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
does not shift the day according to the presenter's computer. A user with no

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
interviews still receives a valid file containing only the headers.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Every value passes through CSV cell escaping. Convert null values to an empty string,

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
and surround each cell with double quotes. Embedded double quotes are doubled, which preserves

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
commas and quotation marks inside company names or addresses. Also prefix suspicious formula-leading characters

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
with an apostrophe. This reduces the risk of a spreadsheet interpreting external data as

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
a formula when the downloaded file is opened.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Join escaped cells with commas and records with carriage return and line feed. The

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
UTF eight byte order mark helps spreadsheet software recognize the encoding. Express sets the

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
content type to text slash CSV and the content disposition to an attachment with

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
the filename my interviews dot CSV. The response is the file itself, not JSON

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
containing a file. Postman can save it with Send and Download.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Here is a real response captured from our local server. Alice has two interviews,

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
and the CSV contains the header plus those two records. The content type and

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
attachment filename are both checked. The saved evidence is in the artifacts directory, and

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
the sample CSV is in delivery. Tokens were deliberately omitted from the evidence files.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
This is a local functional demonstration, not a cloud performance benchmark.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Verification needs more than seeing a successful download. Our tests check the content type,

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
filename, row count, and ownership. Alice's file must not contain Bob's interview. A separate

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
test checks quotation marks and a formula-like company name. Another request omits authentication and

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
must return four oh one. The complete regression collection also verifies booking ownership, date

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
validation, administrator actions, and logout behavior.

46
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
To reproduce the feature, start the backend and run demo requests seventeen and eighteen.

47
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Log in as the student, download the file, and compare it with that student's

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
bookings. The implementation stays within Express, Mongoose, JWT, and the Postman workflow taught in

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
class. The additional work is the personal export and safe CSV serialization. Both team

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
members should review these files and be able to explain the owner filter themselves.
